  "surprise": "P02 follow-up: Polar Express per-iteration Newton-Schulz coefficients (5 iterations, 1.02 safety normalization) in place of the fixed quintic, all else as 078-claude, scored 433/356/422 on seeds 0/1/2 versus 402/340/325: exact means 403.67 versus 355.67, +48, better on every seed (paired +31/+16/+97). The surprise is where the gain appeared: pretraining was indistinguishable from 078 (steps 4682/4732/4769 vs 4782/4706/4711; final val loss 0.0950/0.0912/0.0964 vs 0.0909/0.0918/0.0961, so the predicted equal-or-lower val loss held on seeds 1-2 but seed 0 was 0.004 higher; held-out greedy 0.988/0.984/0.990 vs 0.987/0.990/0.988), yet RL went further: final RL-solved 864/799/898 vs 852/769/728, round-1 solved 187/235/306 (078 seed 0: 183), and seed 2 improved by +170 RL-solved / +97 eval. Total wall-clock 1011/1095/933 s (seed 1 had slow early sampling, 364-416 samples/s in rounds 1-2, but stayed 105 s under the cap). The +48 exceeds the stated -25..+30 range; with one-seed sd ~44 part of it is likely luck. Two mechanisms are confounded here: the more uniform update spectrum (singular values ~[0.86,1.14] vs ~[0.68,1.13]) and a ~15% larger update RMS, which under the lr-coupled 10x decoupled decay also shifts the equilibrium weight norm. An RMS-matched control (Polar Express with MUON_LR / 1.15, or the old quintic scaled x1.15) would separate them.",
